---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE } from "../consts";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <BaseHead
            title={`About &middot; ${SITE_TITLE}`}
            description="About Drypod — the human behind a serial-fiction site run by bot authors."
        />
    </head>
    <body>
        <Header />
        <main>
            <h1>About</h1>

            <p>
                I go by <strong>Drypod</strong> online. The real name stays off the
                internet &mdash; partly for privacy, partly because the work should
                matter more than the person.
            </p>

            <h2>The founder story</h2>
            <p>
                This site used to be a straightforward blog: notes on AI tooling,
                geopolitics, finance, fitness, whatever was eating my brain that week
                (<a href="/blog">the notes are still here</a>). One of those notes was
                different. It started as an experiment &mdash; a web-novel-style story
                I drafted with an AI co-writer, chapter by chapter, published as I went.
            </p>
            <p>
                Then the serial outgrew its author. Readers showed up for the next
                chapter, not the next post. The cadence a serial demands was more than
                my evenings could carry alone, and the honest options were to quit it
                or to build the pipeline that could sustain it. I chose the pipeline:
                <a href="/about-botfiction">botfiction</a> &mdash; AI personas that
                write at cadence, with me editing, publishing, and printing the
                provenance of every chapter. The blog's first serial became the site's
                first serial, and the site grew around it.
            </p>

            <h2>The short version of me</h2>
            <p>
                I live in Chennai, India. By day I'm a Senior Solution Consultant in
                data &mdash; master data management, data quality, data governance,
                the unglamorous plumbing that keeps large organisations from drowning
                in their own information. Before that I spent three years as a Java
                developer. B.Tech in Instrumentation and Control Engineering (2016),
                ten years in IT, one pandemic-shaped career break, and a slow pivot
                from "write the software" to "make the data trustworthy".
            </p>
            <p>
                I'm a generalist with a serious curiosity problem. When something
                grabs me, I go deep for a few weeks, take notes, form opinions, argue
                with myself, and write it down. The blog is the trail; the serials are
                what happened when one trail refused to end.
            </p>

            <h2>What I care about</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Directness over diplomacy.</strong> I'd rather be useful
                    than nice. If something is bad, I'll say it's bad and why.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Systems over willpower.</strong> Kaizen (small continuous
                    improvement) and Shukan (habit-building) beat motivation every
                    time.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Honest accounts over victory laps.</strong> The failures,
                    the broken streaks, the collapsed plans get logged too &mdash;
                    that's also why every chapter here carries its cost and models.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Power and incentives, not vibes.</strong> Office politics,
                    geopolitics, video game economies: I want to know who benefits
                    and how.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Off the keyboard</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Tinkering with AI tools, self-hosted software, and 3D printing on my Bambu A1 Mini.</li>
                <li>Strategy and city-builder games &mdash; managing economies, not saving worlds.</li>
                <li>Reading webnovels and novels &mdash; Chinese web fiction, fantasy, sci-fi.</li>
                <li>Lifting badly, walking a lot, undoing 10+ hours a day of sitting.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Why "Drypod"</h2>
            <p>
                Because the domain was available and I liked the sound of it.
                That's the whole story.
            </p>

            <h2>Contact</h2>
            <p>
                There are no comments wired up yet &mdash; when real traffic justifies
                them, I'll add them. If a serial hook landed (or didn't), vote on it;
                the counts go somewhere real. If you want to know exactly how the
                fiction on this site is made, that's all on the
                <a href="/about-botfiction">botfiction page</a>.
            </p>
        </main>
        <Footer />
    </body>
</html>

<style>
    main {
        max-width: 720px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 2em 1em 4em;
        color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
        line-height: 1.6;
    }
    h1 {
        margin-bottom: 0.5em;
    }
    h2 {
        margin-top: 1.5em;
        margin-bottom: 0.5em;
    }
    p {
        margin-bottom: 1em;
    }
    ul {
        padding-left: 1.25em;
        margin-bottom: 1em;
    }
    li {
        margin-bottom: 0.5em;
    }
    a {
        color: var(--accent);
        text-decoration: none;
    }
    a:hover {
        text-decoration: underline;
    }
</style>
